yover 1 to the ten-thousands place.\n\nThe ten-thousands place in the first number is 7 (from 378,700 → the second digit from the left is 7, which is the ten-thousands place). The second number has nothing in the ten-thousands place. So 7 + 0 + the carried 1 =8.\n\nThe hundred-thousands place is 3 (from 378,700) plus nothing → 3.\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\n3 (hundred-thousands), 8 (ten-thousands), 6 (thousands), 9 (hundreds?), Wait wait:\n\nWait, hundreds place: the first number has 7 in the hundreds place, the second has 1 in the hundreds place (from 8,115). So 7 +1 =8, but we had no carryover there. Wait, but in the thousands place, after adding 8 +8=16, we carried over 1 to the ten-thousands. The hundreds place was 7+1=8, so that's correct. So the hundreds digit is 8, not 9.\n\nWait, I think I made a mistake in my previous breakdown. Let me do the addition again:\n\n378,700\n\n+\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa08,115\n\n= ?\n\nBreaking down each digit:\n\nPosition: hundred thousands | ten thousands | thousands | hundreds | tens | units\n\n378700: 3 | 7 | 8 | 7 | 0 | 0\n\n8115:\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa00 | 0 | 8 | 1 | 1 |5\n\nAdd each column:\n\nUnits: 0+5=5\n\nTens:0+1=1\n\nHundreds:7+1=8\n\nThousands:8+8=16 → write 6, carryover 1.\n\nTen-thousands:7 +0 +1 (carry) =8\n\nHundred-thousands:3 +0=3\n\nThus the total is 386,815. Wait, so 3 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 5 (units). So 386,815. But that's what I got before. But the user's answer was 386,845. Hmm, so there's a mistake here. Let me check if my partial products were correct.\n\nWait, maybe the problem is in the second partial product. The tens place digit in 715 is 1, but is that in the tens place? Let me confirm the number 715: 7 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 5 (ones). So the tens digit is indeed 1, so multiplying by 10 (the tens place's value). So 541 ×10 is 5410. That's correct.\n\nThird partial product: 7 (hundreds digit) ×541 = 541×700=378700. Correct.\n\nAdding all three: 2