reds digit is 6, representing 600). So step 3: 498 × 600 = 298,800.  \n\nThen, sum all three: 1,494 + 44,820 + 298,800. Let me add them again:\n\nFirst, 1,494 + 44,820 = 46,314. Then 46,314 + 298,800 = 345,114. So the answer is 345,114.\n\nTherefore, the user\'s initial thought of 325,114 is incorrect. The correct answer is 345,114. I need to present this in the template format, following the structure given.\n\nThe user\'s example had:\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of <<number1 such as 27>> and <<number2 such as 153>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet\'s think step by step. <<number2 153 has three digits, so that we can reason in three steps.>>\n1. <<Multiply number1 27 by the ones place digit 3 of number2 153>>\n2. <<Multiply number1 27 by the tens place digit 50 of number2 153>>\n<<other digits of number2 if it has>>\nAnswer:\nNow, sum all the step results: <<sum of the results>>.\nSo, the final computed product is <<answer>>.\n\nSo applying that to 498 and 693:\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 498 and 693?\n# Reasoning:\nLet\'s think step by step. 693 has three digits, so we can reason in three steps.\n1. Multiply 498 by the ones place digit 3 of 693: 498 × 3 = 1,494.\n2. Multiply 498 by the tens place digit 90 (since the tens digit is 9, so 9×10) of 693: 498 × 90 = 44,820.\n3. Multiply 498 by the hundreds place digit 600 (since the hundreds digit is 6, so 6×100) of 693: 498 × 600 = 298,800.\nAnswer:\nNow, sum all the step results: 1,494 + 44,820 + 298,800 = 345,114.\nSo, the final computed product is 345,114.\n\nTherefore, the user\'s answer of 325,114 is incorrect. The correct answer is 345,114. I need to make sure that in the template, the steps are clearly laid out as per the original structure.\n</think>', ''] 14%|#4        | 70/500 [1:45:57<10:28:48, 87.74s/it]
