hat\'s correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user did 536 ×157 but miscalculated each step.\n\nWait, maybe they did 536×157 as:\n\nFirst step: 536 ×7 = 3,752 (correct)\n\nSecond step: 536 ×5 (tens place, so actually ×50). Wait, if they forgot the shift, they might have done 536×5 =2,680 and added it as is, so 3,752 +2,680 =6,432. Then third step 536×1 (hundreds place, so ×100) 53,600. Adding that gives 6,432 +53,600 =60,032. Still not 24k.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they multiplied 536 ×157 as (500+36)×(100+50+7), but made an error in cross terms.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps they thought 536 ×157 = (500 ×100) + (30×50) + (6×7) =50,000 +1,500 +42=51,542? Still not 24k.\n\nHmm, maybe they did 536×15 instead of 157? 536×15 is 8,040. Not 24k. Alternatively, 536×45? No.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought 536×157 is 500×150 =75,000 plus 36×7=252, totaling 75,252? Still not matching.\n\nWait the user\'s answer is 24,152. Let\'s see what 536 ×45 would be? 536×45=24,120, which is close but not exactly. Alternatively, 536 ×45 is 24,120. Maybe they did 536×45 by mistake? But the problem says 157. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps they miscalculated the hundreds place step. Let me see:\n\nIf they did 536 ×1 (hundreds place digit 1) but didn\'t multiply by 100, so just 536×1=536, then total would be 3,752 (from 7) + 26,800 (from 50) +536 (from 1 instead of 100) would be 3,752 +26,800 =30,552 +536 =31,088. Still not 24k.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they multiplied 536 ×157 as (500×100) + (30×50) + (6×7) plus cross terms but messed up?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps they did 536 ×157 as (500 + 36) × (100 +50 +7) and miscalculated terms. Let me try:\n\n(500+36)(100+50+7) =500×100 +500×50 +500×7 +36×100 +36×50 +36×7\n\n=50,000 +25,000 +3,500 +3,600 +1,800 +252\n\nAdding these:\n\n50,000 +25,000 =75,000\n\n75,000 +3,500 =78,500\n\n78,500 +3,600 =82,100\n\n82,100 +1,800 =83,900\n\n83,900 +252 =84,152. So that\'s correct again.\n\nHmm, so maybe the user\'s answer is wrong. The correct answer should be