final_output:  ['171,836? Wait, wait, let me check the addition again. Maybe I made a mistake here. Let me recalculate the total sum step by step.\n\nFirst part: 2,166 (6×361).\n\nSecond part: 25,270 (70×361).\n\nThird part: 144,400 (400×361).\n\nAdding first two: 2,166 +25,270. Let\'s do 2,166 +25,270. \n\n2,166 +25,270:\n\n2,000 +25,000 =27,000\n\n166 +270 =436\n\nTotal is 27,000 +436 =27,436.\n\nNow add 27,436 +144,400:\n\n27,436 +144,400. \n\n27,000 +144,400 =171,400\n\nThen add the remaining 436: 171,400 +436 =171,836.\n\nWait, but the user thought the answer was 171,832. Hmm. So there must be an error in my calculation somewhere. Let me check each step again carefully.\n\nStarting with 361 ×6: 361×6. Let me compute this again.\n\n361 ×6: 300×6=1800, 60×6=360, 1×6=6. So 1800+360 is 2160 +6 is 2166. That\'s correct.\n\nNext, 361×70. Let\'s compute 361×7 first. 300×7=2100, 60×7=420, 1×7=7. So 2100+420=2520 +7=2527. Then ×10 gives 25,270. That\'s correct.\n\nThen 361×400: 361×4=1,444, so ×100 is 144,400. That\'s correct.\n\nAdding them all: 2,166 +25,270 =27,436. Then 27,436 +144,400.\n\n27,436 +144,400:\n\nLet me add 27,436 + 144,400:\n\nStart with 144,400 +20,000 =164,400\n\nThen add 7,436: 164,400 +7,000 =171,400, then +436 =171,836.\n\nHmm. So according to my calculation, it\'s 171,836, but the user says they think it\'s 171,832. So there\'s a discrepancy here. I must have made a mistake somewhere.\n\nWait, let me check the multiplication steps again. Let me try a different approach. Maybe using the standard algorithm.\n\nMultiplying 361 ×476.\n\nFirst, write 361\n\n×476\n\n________\n\nStart with the ones digit (6):\n\n361 ×6: \n\n1×6=6\n\n6×6=36, carryover 3?\n\nWait, actually, step by step:\n\n361  \n× 6  \n=?\n\nUnits place: 1×6=6\n\nTens place: 6×6=36, write down 6 and carryover 3.\n\nHundreds place: 3×6=18 plus the carried over 3 is 21. So total is 2166. Correct.\n\nNext, the tens digit (7), but shifted one position:\n\n361 ×70:\n\nSo 361×7=2527, then add a zero gives 25,270. Correct.\n\nThen the hundreds digit (4), shifted two positions:\n\n361 ×400:\n\n361×4=1,444, then add two zeros gives 144,400. Correct.\n\nAdding all three: 2,166 +25,270 =27,436; then 27,436 +144,400 =171,836.\n\nHmm, so the user\'s answer is 171,832. Maybe they made a mistake? Or perhaps I made an error?\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should compute using another method to verify.\n\nLet me do 361 ×476 using another approach. Let\'s use distributive property:\n\n361×476 = 361×(400 +70 +6) = 361×400 +361×70 +361×6, which is exactly what I did before, so that\'s correct.\n\nAlternatively, let me compute 361 ×476 step by step:\n\nAlternatively, compute 476 ×361:\n\n476 ×300 = 142,800\n\n476 ×60 =28,560\n\n476 ×1 =476\n\nAdding those: 142,800 +28,560 =171,360; then +476 =171,836.\n\nSame result. So the correct answer is 171,836. Therefore the user\'s answer of 171,832 is incorrect. Maybe they had a miscalculation in their steps.\n\nWait, let me check their thought process. They might have done something wrong in their own steps. Let me see:\n\nSuppose in the addition steps, maybe they added 2166 +25,270 first. Let\'s see:\n\n2166 +25,270:\n\n2166 +25,270. Let\'s add the numbers:\n\n25,270\n\n+ 2,166\n\n=27,436. That\'s correct.\n\nThen 27,436 +144,400:\n\n27,436 +144,400. Let\'s add:\n\n144,400\n\n+27,436\n\n=171,836. So same as before.\n\nHmm. So the user\'s answer is different. Maybe they made a mistake in one of the multiplication steps.\n\nWait, maybe the original numbers are different? The user wrote "the product of 361 and 476" and their answer is 171,832. But according to my calculations, it\'s 171,836. The difference is 4. Let me see where that 4 could come from.\n\nSuppose in the 361 ×70 step, maybe they got 25,266 instead of 25,270? Let me check:\n\nIf 361×70 was miscalculated as 25,266, then adding:\n\n2166 +25,266 =27,432. Then 27,432 +144,400 =171,832. That would be the case if they had an error in the 70×361 step.\n\nLet me see: 361 ×7:\