n"I think the correct answer is: 430738"\n\nSo 430,738 versus my 432,738. The difference is exactly 2,000. Hmm. Let me see if I made a mistake in the addition steps.\n\nWait when adding 18,238 +414,500:\n\n18,238 + 414,500. Let\'s break it down:\n\n414,500 + 18,000 = 432,500\n\nThen add 238: 432,500 +238 =432,738. That\'s correct.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps in the first step I miscalculated 829×20?\n\nWait 829×20 is 829×2×10=1,658×10=16,580. Correct.\n\nHmm. Alternatively, perhaps the user intended 829×522 but made an arithmetic mistake. Alternatively, maybe I misread the numbers. Let me check the problem again: "What is the product of 829 and 522?" So yes, the numbers are correct.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I should do the multiplication another way to confirm.\n\nLet me compute 829 × 522 using another method.\n\nBreak down 522 into 500 + 20 +2.\n\nSo 829×500 = 414,500\n\n829×20 = 16,580\n\n829×2 =1,658\n\nAdding them: 414,500 +16,580 =431,080; then +1,658 gives 432,738. Same as before.\n\nHmm. So unless the user made an error in their calculation, the correct answer is 432,738. But they thought it was 430,738. Maybe they did 829×522 as follows: perhaps they messed up the hundreds place?\n\nWait, maybe in their calculation they did 829×500 as 410,000 instead of 414,500. Let\'s see: 829×500. If someone does 800×500=400,000 and 29×500=14,500. 400k+14.5k is 414,500. So that\'s correct. If they messed up the 29×500 as 14,000 instead, then total would be 414,000. Then 414,000+16,580=430,580, plus 1,658 would be 432,238. Hmm, not exactly. Alternatively, maybe they subtracted?\n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 829×522 as (800+29)(500+22). Let\'s see:\n\n(800+29)(500+22) = 800×500 +800×22 +29×500 +29×22\n\n=400,000 +17,600 +14,500 +638\n\nAdding those: 400k +17,600 =417,600; +14,500 =432,100; +638=432,738. Same result.\n\nHmm. I think my calculation is correct, so the user might have made an error. Therefore, the correct answer should be 432,738. But the user\'s answer is dif