nce of 300. Let me check again:\n\nWait, 650 * 261:\n\nLet me do it step by step again:\n\n650 × 261:\n\nBreakdown:\n\nFirst, 650 × 1 = 650\n\nSecond, 650 × 60 = 39,000 (since 650×6=3,900, times 10 is 39,000)\n\nThird, 650 × 200 = 130,000\n\nAdding them up: 650 + 39,000 = 39,650. Then 39,650 + 130,000 = 169,650.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user multiplied 650 by 261 incorrectly. Let me check using another method:\n\n261 × 650 can also be written as (260 +1) × (600 +50) ?\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should use the standard multiplication vertically:\n\n       650\n     ×261\n     -------\n       650   (650 × 1)\n     39000   (650 × 60, shifted one position)\n    130000   (650 × 200, shifted two positions)\n    ---------\n    169650\n\nYes, that\'s 169,650. So unless there\'s a miscalculation here, the correct answer should be 169,650. Therefore, the user might have made a mistake in their own calculation. Let me see where they might have gone wrong. If they thought the answer was 169,950, perhaps they added an extra 300. Maybe in the second step, 650×60, they got 39,300 instead of 39,000? Let me see:\n\nIf 650×60 were miscalculated as (650×6)=3,900, then ×10 is 39,000. So that\'s correct. Alternatively, if they did 650×60 as 650×6×10, but maybe they thought 650×6 is 3,900 + 300? Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they multiplied 650 by 261 as follows:\n\n261 × 600 = 156,600\n\n261 × 50 = 13,050\n\nAdding those gives 156,600 + 13,050 = 169,650. So same result. So I think the user\'s answer is incorrect. The correct product is 169,650. Therefore, I should point that out in the reasoning, explaining the steps and showing where the discrepancy might be.\n</think>', '']
