But if they thought the tens digit was 10 (i.e. 10\u00d710?), but no. Alternatively, maybe the original numbers were different? Wait, the user's question is about 566 and 214, so the numbers are correct. Hmm. Alternatively, maybe they thought the hundreds digit was 2000? Wait, no, 214 is 2 hundreds, 1 ten, 4 ones.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake. Let me do 566 \u00d7214 using another method. Let's do standard multiplication:\n\n     566\n\n   \u00d7214\n\n   --------\n\n     2264  (566 \u00d74)\n\n    5660   (566 \u00d710, shifted one position)\n\n  113200  (566 \u00d7200, shifted two positions)\n\nAdding those:\n\n2264\n\n+5660 \u2192 7924\n\n+113200 \u2192 total is 121,124. So that's correct.\n\nTherefore, the user's answer is wrong, and the correct answer is 121,124. So I need to present the correct steps leading to that answer.\n</think>\n\n",
