ke the distributive property.\n\nAlternatively, compute 628*561 as (600+28)*(500+60+1). Maybe expanding this will help.\n\nFirst, 600*500 = 300,000\n\n600*60 = 36,000\n\n600*1 = 600\n\n28*500 = 14,000\n\n28*60 = 1,680\n\n28*1 =28\n\nNow add all these:\n\n300,000 + 36,000 = 336,000\n\n336,000 + 600 = 336,600\n\n336,600 +14,000 = 350,600\n\n350,600 +1,680 = 352,280\n\n352,280 +28 = 352,308.\n\nSame result again. So perhaps the user made a mistake in their calculation? Let me check the user\'s supposed answer: 352,305 versus my result 352,308. The difference is 3. Where could that come from?\n\nWait, maybe I made an error in one of the steps. Let me check each component again:\n\nFirst component: 628*1=628. Correct.\n\nSecond component: 628*60=37,680. Let me recompute 628*60:\n\n628 * 60 = 628*(6*10) = (628*6)*10. 628*6: 600*6=3,600; 28*6=168. 3,600+168=3,768. *10 is 37,680. Correct.\n\nThird component: 628*500=314,000. Yes, because 628*5=3,140, times 100 is 314,000. \n\nAdding them: 314,000 +37,680 = 351,680. Then plus 628 gives 352,308. So yes, that\'s right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user did 628*561 as follows: perhaps miscalculating one of the steps. Let me see: if they thought 628*6 was 3,765 instead of 3,768, then 3,765*10 would be 37,650. Then adding to 314,000 + 37,650 = 351,650 +628 = 352,278. Not quite.\n\nAlternatively, maybe 628*500 was done as 628*5=3,140, but then times 100 would be 314,000. Hmm. Alternatively, perhaps they added wrong. Let me see:\n\nSuppose they added 314,000 + 37,680 first. 314,000 + 37,680. 314k + 30k is 344k, then 7,680 more would be 351,680. Then adding 628 gives 351,680 +628 = 352,308. So that\'s correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user thought that 628*561 is 628*(560+1) = 628*560 +628. 628*560: 628*500=314,000; 628*60=37,680. So 314k +37,680 = 351,680. Then +628 gives 352,308. So same.\n\nHmm. Unless there was a miscalculation in the user\'s thought process. Alternatively, maybe the numbers were different? Wait, the origina