183 directly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should do 817×183 as (800+17)×(100+80+3). But that might be more complicated.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in the multiplication steps. Let me try multiplying 817 by 183 using another method.\n\nLet me write it out vertically:\n\n\u2003\u2003817  \n×\xa0183  \n—————  \nFirst, multiply by 3: 817×3=2451  \nThen multiply by 80: 817×80=65,360  \nThen multiply by 100: 817×100=81,700  \nAdding them: 2451 +65,360=67,811; then 67,811 +81,700=149,511. So that's the same result. So the correct answer should be 149,511, not 149,611. Therefore the user's thought answer is incorrect. Maybe they miscalculated somewhere.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made an error in my own calculation. Let me check again:\n\nWait, perhaps when adding 67,811 +81,700, maybe I messed up the numbers. Let's do it digit by digit:\n\n67,811  \n+81,700  \n=?\n\nStart from the right:\n\nOnes place:1+0=1  \nTens:1+0=1  \nHundreds:8+7=15, carryover 1  \nThousands:7 (from first number's thousands place is 7 in 67,811? Wait, let's see:\n\nWait, 67,811 is written as:\n\nDigits: 6 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\n81,700 is 8 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nAdding:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:1+0=1\n\nTens:1+0=1\n\nHundreds:8 +7=15, write down 5, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place:7 (from first number) +1 (from second number) + carried over 1 = 9.\n\nTen-thousands place:6 (from first) +8 (from second) =14.\n\nSo the total is 14 (ten-thousands), 9 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 1 (ones). So 149,511. Yes, that's correct. So the user's answer of 149,611 must be wrong. Maybe they added an extra 100 somewhere?\n\nAlternatively, maybe in one of the partial products they had an error. Let me check their thought process. The user said the answer is 149,611, but according to my calculations, it's 149,511. The difference is 100. Let's see where that could come in.\n\nWait, maybe when they m