swer with proper steps.\n\nWait, let me check once more with another method. Let\'s do 218 ×561:\n\nBreakdown:\n\n218 ×561 = (200 +18) ×(500 +60 +1)\n\nBut that might complicate, but let\'s see:\n\nFirst, 200×500 =100,000\n\n200×60=12,000\n\n200×1=200\n\n18×500=9,000\n\n18×60=1,080\n\n18×1=18\n\nNow add all these:\n\n100,000 +12,000 =112,000\n\n+200 →112,200\n\n+9,000 →121,200\n\n+1,080 →122,280\n\n+18 →122,298. Yep, same result.\n\nOkay, so definitely the correct answer is 122,298. The user\'s thought of 192,298 is wrong. So I need to present the correct steps as per the template.\n\nThe user\'s question is in the format where they want me to fill in the template. The original template example had:\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 27 and 153?\n# Reasoning:\nLet\'s think step by step. 153 has three digits, so that we can reason in three steps.\n1. Multiply 27 by the ones place digit 3 of 153: 27 × 3 = 81\n2. Multiply 27 by the tens place digit 50 of 153: 27 × 50 = 1350\n3. Multiply 27 by the hundreds place digit 100 of 153: 27 × 100 = 2700\nAnswer: Now, sum all the step results: 81 + 1350 + 2700 = 4131.\nSo, the final computed product is 4131.\n\nSo following that structure, for the given problem with 218 and 561, which has three digits, the steps would be:\n\n1. Multiply 218 by the ones digit (1) → 218×1=218\n\n2. Multiply 218 by the tens digit (60) → 218×60=13,080\n\n3. Multiply 218 by the hundreds digit (500) → 218×500=109,000\n\nThen sum them: 218 +13,080 =13,298 +109,000=122,298.\n\nSo that\'s the correct process. Therefore, the final answer is 122,298, not 192,298. The user might have made an error in their calculation, perhaps miscalculating one of the multiplications or the addition.\n\nAlright, I need to structure this into the template properly.\n</think>', ''] 59%|#####9    | 296/500 [7:02:55<4:39:41, 82.26s/it]
