more digits, write 45. .\nSo that gives 4554. But since this is the hundreds place, we need to shift two positions to the left, so actually, it's 4554 multiplied by 100, but in terms of writing the partial products, it would be 455400? Wait, maybe I should think of it step by step. .\n\nWait, actually, in the standard multiplication setup, each subsequent partial product is shifted left by one more digit. So the first (ones place) is 506, the second (tens place) would be 0 shifted one, so 0, and the third (hundreds place) would be 4554 shifted two places, so 455400. .\n\nThen, adding all the partial products: 506 (from ones) + 0 (from tens) + 455400 (from hundreds)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
