458; 17,458+203,000=220,458. So that's correct. So the correct answer is 220,458. But the user thought it was 220,478. Hmm. Maybe they added wrong in their steps. Let me see if there's another way they might have miscalculated. .\n\nWait, maybe in the first step, 406\u00d73 is 1218. That's correct. Second step: 406\u00d740 is 16,240. Third step: 406\u00d7500 is 203,000. Adding 1218+16,240 is 17,458. Then 17,458 +203,000 is 220,458. So the user's answer is off by 20. Perhaps in the hundreds place multiplication, they did 406\u00d7500 as 203,020? That would add 20 more. Or maybe they did 406\u00d75=2030, then times 100 is 203,000. Hmm. Alternatively, maybe they added 16,240 +203,000 first? Let's see: 16,240 +203,000 is 219,240. Then adding 1,218 would give 220,458. Still the same. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought 406\u00d7543 as (400+6)(500+40+3). Let me try expanding that. \n\nUsing distributive property:\n\n(400 +6)(500 +40 +3) = 400\u00d7500 +400\u00d740 +400\u00d73 +6\u00d7500 +6\u00d740 +6\u00d73.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n400\u00d7500 = 200,000.\n\n400\u00d740 =16,000.\n\n400\u00d73=1,200.\n\n6\u00d7500=3,000.\n\n6\u00d740=240.\n\n6\u00d73=18.\n\nNow add all these together:\n\n200,000 +16,000 =216,000.\n\n216,000 +1,200 =217,200.\n\n217,200 +3,000 =220,200.\n\n220,200 +240 =220,440.\n\n220,440 +18 =220,458. Same result. So that's correct. \n\nHmm, so the user's answer of 220,478 is 20 more. Let me see where that could come from. Maybe they added 1218 +16,240 incorrectly. Let me check that again. 1218 +16,240. 1218 +16,000 =17,218. Then +240 is 17,458. That's correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought 406\u00d740 is 16,240, but 406\u00d740 is indeed 16,240. Alternatively, maybe they did 406\u00d7543 as 406\u00d7500 + 406\u00d743. Let's try that. \n\n406\u00d7543 =406\u00d7(500+43)=406\u00d7500 +406\u00d743.\n\n406\u00d7500 is 203,000.\n\nNow 406\u00d743. Let's compute that:\n\n406\u00d740 =16,240.\n\n406\u00d73=1,218.\n\nAdding those gives 16,240 +1,2